# CPython 3.15t OPTIONAL feature patch: thread-state EXECUTION home
#   build flag:  -DPy_TSTATE_EXEC_HOME      (OFF by default)
#                -DPy_TSTATE_TLBC_FOLLOW    (with it; item 7)
#   pinned to:   Python-3.15.0  (applies at ZERO fuzz: patch -p1 -F0; so does
#                rc3.  NOT to rc1/rc2 -- see item 6; their copy is in git history)
#   series pair: this is the EXECUTION half for the 3.15 series.  The matching
#                ALLOCATION half is cpython315t-tstate-alloc-home.patch; migration
#                needs BOTH.  Port of cpython314t-tstate-exec-home.patch -- NOT
#                interchangeable (the 3.14 patch rejects on 3.15).
#
# Same feature, flag, _Py_TID_ASM witness and lock hunks (lock.c, parking_lot.c,
# _threadmodule.c, import.c, bufferedio.c, semaphore.c: locks owned by a
# fiber's thread state, and their waits able to park a fiber) as the 3.14
# patch -- see
# cpython314t-tstate-exec-home.patch for the full WHAT/WHY, codegen evidence,
# measured cost, and caveats (no LTO -- PGO alone is fine; do not annotate
# _PyThreadState_GetCurrent() pure/const; MSVC intrinsics not covered).  All of
# it applies here unchanged.
#
# WHAT CHANGED vs the 3.14 patch -- purely structural, no semantic change
# (bar item 6, which only touches MinGW ARM64):
#
# 1. _Py_ThreadId() MOVED.  3.15 relocated it (and _Py_GetThreadLocal_Addr) from
#    Include/object.h to Include/cpython/object.h, and dropped the
#    `&& !defined(Py_LIMITED_API)` from its `#if defined(Py_GIL_DISABLED)` guard.
#    So this patch targets Include/cpython/object.h.  The asm templates are
#    byte-identical to 3.14, so the transformation is the same: define _Py_TID_ASM
#    (volatile iff Py_TSTATE_EXEC_HOME) and route every thread-pointer READ through
#    it.  The `register uintptr_t tp __asm__ ("r13")` / `("r2")` constraints are
#    left alone -- bindings, not reads.
#
# 2. _PyThreadState_GET() guard simplified.  3.15's guard is
#    `#if !defined(Py_BUILD_CORE_MODULE)` (3.14 also required HAVE_THREAD_LOCAL),
#    so the added `&& !defined(Py_TSTATE_EXEC_HOME)` clause attaches to the new
#    form.  Semantics identical: under exec-home the read goes out-of-line.
#
# 3. Python/pystate.c -- the Py_NO_INLINE + comment on _PyThreadState_GetCurrent()
#    is unchanged from the 3.14 patch and applies to 3.15 as-is.
#
# 4. Objects/memoryobject.c -- the atomic managed-buffer export count (the 3.14
#    patch's "ALSO" section): 3.15.0rc3 has the same plain ++/-- on
#    _PyManagedBufferObject.exports at the same lines.  The 3.14 patch's other
#    memoryobject.c change, backporting FT_ATOMIC_ADD_SSIZE to the per-view
#    count in memoryview.hex()/memory_hash(), is not needed: 3.15 has it.  The
#    same _Py_MV_EXPORTS_ATOMIC witness is defined next to _Py_TID_ASM.
#
# 5. Modules/arraymodule.c -- the atomic array.array export count (also the
#    3.14 patch's "ALSO" section): 3.15.0rc3 has the same plain ++/-- in
#    array_buffer_getbuf/relbuf and the same three "is it exporting?" reads,
#    so the hunk is the 3.14 one at shifted lines.  Its witness,
#    _Py_ARRAY_EXPORTS_ATOMIC, sits next to _Py_MV_EXPORTS_ATOMIC.
#
# 6. MinGW ARM64 read (new in 3.15.0rc3).  gh-157212 replaced that branch's
#    `tid = __getReg(18);` intrinsic with an inline-asm read of x18, which is
#    the context line of the _Py_ThreadId hunk -- hence no rc1/rc2.  The new
#    read is a thread-pointer read like the rest, so it goes through
#    _Py_TID_ASM too; only MSVC's (and MinGW x86/x64's) intrinsics stay
#    uncovered.  stackweave does not build on Windows, so this one is for
#    consistency, not something it relies on.
#
# 7. Thread-local bytecode follows the thread state (Python/ceval_macros.h,
#    Python/ceval.c; own flag -DPy_TSTATE_TLBC_FOLLOW, as for 3.14 -- see
#    its ALSO section).  3.15 adds DISPATCH_NON_TRACING() and the tracing
#    dispatch tables, and passes instruction_funcptr_table through the
#    tail-call JUMP_TO_PREDICTED; the hunk follows in each the same way.  The
#    3.15.0 instruction tails after an escaping call touch the same bytecode
#    as 3.14.4's (a callee's function version, branch-history bits, the
#    specializer itself) plus JIT-only paths a free-threaded build lacks.
#    3.15 also compiles a second eval loop from ceval_macros.h
#    (Modules/_testinternalcapi/interpreter.c); only ceval.c defines
#    _PY_TLBC_FOLLOW_LABEL and the shared slow path, so that one follows
#    inline.
#
# 8. Fiber preemption at the eval breaker (the 3.14 patch's "ALSO -- fiber
#    preemption" section): the same hunks.  3.15 moved the async-exception
#    branch of _Py_HandlePending() into _PyEval_RaiseAsyncExc(), which
#    PyErr_CheckSignals() also calls -- so a C loop that checks for signals
#    (_sre every 4096 steps) cleared the borrowed _PY_ASYNC_EXCEPTION_BIT before
#    the eval loop could see it, and a fiber in such a loop was almost never
#    preempted.  _PyEval_RaiseAsyncExc() therefore leaves the bit set while the
#    thread state's _fiber_break request is pending; the end of
#    _Py_HandlePending() takes the request and clears it, once
#    _fiber_break_hold (raised around the pending calls and a GC collection)
#    is down.  Witness: _Py_FIBER_BREAKER_HOOK in Include/cpython/ceval.h.
#
# ZERO IMPACT BY DEFAULT: with the flag undefined _Py_TID_ASM expands to plain
# __asm__, _PyThreadState_GET() keeps its inline thread-local read, and the
# export counts keep their plain ++/--.  No public ABI change (as for 3.14).
